  "topics": [
    {
      "key": "heritage_reconstruction",
      "title": "[DEMO] How should damaged heritage be reconstructed when authenticity, community needs and future use conflict?",
      "description": "FLAGSHIP SIMULATION — no initial position is implied.\n\nContext: World Heritage practice treats authenticity, documentation, conservation and cultural context as central, while reconstruction debates increasingly consider community and intangible values alongside material evidence.\n\nTrigger: A fictional heritage property suffers major damage. Documentation is strong for some elements but incomplete for others, while the surrounding community seeks rapid restoration of use.\n\nDecision task: Agree on what may be reconstructed, what must remain legible as loss, documentation thresholds, community participation and a staged conservation plan.\n\nCommon evidence baseline: Operational Guidelines for the Implementation of the World Heritage Convention (2025); Authenticity in the context of the World Heritage Convention: Evolution and Impact; Reconstruction: changing attitudes.\n\nStructured casting, evidence URLs, uncertainties, event injects, round plan and cross-World links are in the seed flagship source file.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "built-heritage"
    },
    {
      "key": "living_heritage_safeguarding",
      "title": "[DEMO] How should institutions safeguard living heritage without freezing or appropriating it?",
      "description": "Deliberate cultural change, community agency, ethics, law and public policy.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "living-heritage"
    },
    {
      "key": "heritage_tourism_capacity",
      "title": "[DEMO] How should heritage destinations manage tourism pressure while sustaining local benefit?",
      "description": "Compare cultural impacts, economics, management, planning and environmental pressures.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "cultural-policy"
    },
    {
      "key": "digital_cultural_collections",
      "title": "[DEMO] What access model should govern digitized cultural collections and documentary heritage?",
      "description": "FLAGSHIP SIMULATION — no initial position is implied.\n\nContext: UNESCO’s Memory of the World programme combines preservation and access. Its normative framework recognizes digital continuity, technological obsolescence, rights-holder interests and public value.\n\nTrigger: A fictional consortium of memory institutions must publish a large digitized collection while replacing an obsolete repository platform.\n\nDecision task: Define preservation responsibilities, access tiers, rights/context controls, interoperability, migration policy and long-term stewardship.\n\nCommon evidence baseline: Memory of the World: About the programme; Recommendation concerning the preservation of, and access to, documentary heritage including in digital form; Monitoring of the 2015 documentary heritage Recommendation.\n\nStructured casting, evidence URLs, uncertainties, event injects, round plan and cross-World links are in the seed flagship source file.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "digital-heritage"
    }
  ],
